From 108d135f4bc316f077eae39b3126936807eb0a16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 09:14:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/16] hw/i2c: model the BCM2835 SPI/BSC slave, replacing the
 unimplemented stub

BSC_SL_OFFSET was create_unimp("bcm2835-spis"), so the one peripheral
that makes a Pi an I2C target was unreachable.

Modelled from the BCM2835 ARM Peripherals datasheet section 11, which
specifies every bit of CR, FR, RSR, DR, IFLS and IMSC.  Linux has no
driver for this block -- not upstream and not in the Raspberry Pi
vendor tree -- so the datasheet is the only source, and it is complete.

It is modelled as a QEMU I2CSlave rather than a sysbus device, so it
sits on a real bus with a real master driving it, and is attached to
BSC0 -- the master the ARM can reach.  Wiring a master's pins to the
slave's is a standard bring-up arrangement on a board, and this is that
arrangement.  The point of doing it this way is that the master side is
hw/i2c/bcm2835_i2c.c, which is not part of this series: a byte that
arrives here has been through code we did not write.  Nothing in that
file was touched to make this work.

Three datasheet behaviours that are easy to get backwards, modelled as
specified rather than as convenient:

  RSR's two error bits are cleared by writing ZERO to them.  Every
  other error bit on this SoC is write-one-to-clear, so the usual idiom
  leaves the error set forever while appearing to clear it.

  A disabled block does not answer its address at all -- match_and_add
  returns false rather than the device NAKing -- so a master sees no
  device rather than a broken one.

  DR carries the FIFO levels, busy flags and both error bits alongside
  the data byte, and FR reports the same flags at different bit
  positions.

Not modelled, and logged rather than silently accepted: SPI slave mode,
because QEMU has no peripheral-side SSI abstraction to attach it to.
The GPU status and host control registers, and the ENSTAT/ENCTRL
protocol variants that transfer them as a first data character, are
storage -- they serve the VideoCore boot path, which has no counterpart
here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
